An empty archive is not an argument error, and raising for it aborted a whole drawRect.

-[NSUnarchiver initForReadingWithData:] asserted data.length > 0. Swift Publisher keeps a colour in
its user defaults as archived data and reads it back on EVERY document draw, so the raise unwound
out of -[CCDocView drawRect:] before it had drawn anything, 277 times in one run, and the only
evidence was a single caught-exception line with no caller in it.

CIDER_TRACE_EXCEPTIONS gave the whole chain in one run, which is what that switch is for:

  -[CCDocView drawRect:] -> CFTLayoutGuideline::Draw -> +[CCEnvironment unlockedLayoutGuidelineColor]
    -> -[NSUserDefaults(myColorSupport) my_colorForKey:] -> +[NSUnarchiver unarchiveObjectWithData:]
    -> -[NSUnarchiver initForReadingWithData:] -> NSParameterAssert -> raise

THE DATA IS EMPTY BECAUSE WE WROTE IT EMPTY, and that is the real defect, recorded separately:
-[NSArchiver encodeRootObject:] IS AN EMPTY METHOD, and every encode primitive under it,
encodeObject:, encodeValueOfObjCType:at:, encodeArrayOfObjCType:count:at:, encodeBytes:length:, is
an NSLog stub. So +[NSArchiver archivedDataWithRootObject:] returns ZERO BYTES for anything, and
FTUnlockedLayoutGuidelineColor in the application's plist is an empty NSData, confirmed by reading
the file. Every application in this port that archives an object into its defaults stores nothing.

Answering nil lets a caller that checks, and this one does, fall back to its default, which is the
same rule as the nil path guard fixed in 0078: a branch that reports a bad argument must not raise
into an application that will have the exception swallowed.

MEASURED: 277 caught exceptions before, 0 after.

HONEST LIMIT: the two runs after the fix both ended on Swift Publisher's trial prompt, which appears
periodically, so they do not prove the document draw was exercised as heavily as the failing run
did. The fix itself is unconditional and cannot vary with timing, and the before number was taken
with the stack that names CCDocView.

--- a/src/NSUnarchiver.m
+++ b/src/NSUnarchiver.m
@@ -106,7 +106,26 @@
 
 - (id)initForReadingWithData:(NSData*)data
 {
-    NSParameterAssert(data.length > 0);
+    /*
+     * EMPTY IS NOT AN ARGUMENT ERROR HERE, IT IS AN EMPTY ARCHIVE, and raising for it aborted a
+     * whole drawRect.
+     *
+     * Swift Publisher keeps a colour in its defaults as archived data and reads it back on EVERY
+     * document draw. Our NSArchiver writes NOTHING (encodeRootObject: is an empty method and the
+     * whole encoding layer below it is NSLog stubs), so the stored value is zero bytes, the read
+     * raised, and -[CCDocView drawRect:] unwound before it had drawn anything: 277 times in one
+     * run, with the document area left unpainted and the only evidence a single caught-exception
+     * line. The write side is the real defect and has its own task.
+     *
+     * Answering nil lets a caller that checks, and this one does, fall back to its default. That
+     * is the same rule as the nil path guard in attributesOfItemAtPath: a branch that reports a
+     * bad argument must not raise into an application that will have the exception swallowed.
+     */
+    if (data.length == 0)
+    {
+        [self release];
+        return nil;
+    }
     
     if(self = [super init])
     {
